---
import CaseStudyLayout from '@components/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StatCallout from '@components/StatCallout.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="SP500 vs. Stock Picker"
  subtitle="Backtesting framework that quantifies the long-term cost of active stock picking versus passive S&P 500 index investing — with Monte Carlo simulation to surface the full distribution of outcomes."
  category="Machine Learning · Financial Analytics"
  tools={['Python', 'pandas', 'yfinance', 'numpy', 'Plotly', 'Monte Carlo']}
  duration="2024 – ongoing"
  ogDescription="Data-driven comparison of active stock picking vs. S&P 500 passive investing using backtesting and Monte Carlo simulation."
>

  <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Objective</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">What would have happened if you'd just bought the index?</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Most retail investors underperform the S&P 500 over any 10-year period, due to various 
        transaction costs, timing mistakes, concentration risk, and anchoring to individual positions. 
        This tool makes that cost concrete. Given any portfolio of individual stocks and a date range, 
        it computes exactly what you'd have had if you'd put the same dollars into SPY instead.
        Many tools tell you returns based on a single time investment in a stock at a prior date. 
        This lets you put in your whole stock purchase history and recalculates performance based on
        all those buys as if you would have bought S&P 500 instead. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is a data-driven financial literacy tool and answers
         the question: "what was the opportunity cost of your stock picks?"
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- DATA SOURCES -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Data Sources</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Historical price data via yfinance</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Price history for individual stocks and the S&P 500 index (via the SPY ETF as
        benchmark) is fetched from Yahoo Finance using the <span class="cs-code-inline">yfinance</span> Python
        library. Adjusted close prices are used throughout — this accounts for dividends
        and stock splits, which significantly affect long-run return calculations.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tool supports arbitrary tickers and date ranges. Data is cached locally after
        the first fetch to avoid re-downloading on repeated runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- METHODOLOGY -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Methodology</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Backtesting framework and Monte Carlo outcome simulation</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        The analysis has two components: a deterministic backtest for any specific portfolio,
        and a Monte Carlo simulation for forward-looking scenario analysis.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Backtesting Engine</h3>
      <p>
        For a given portfolio (tickers, weights, start date), the backtest computes the
        daily portfolio value alongside the SPY benchmark, assuming the same initial
        capital and no rebalancing. Key metrics — CAGR, Sharpe ratio, max drawdown, and
        time underwater — are computed for both portfolios.
      </p>
    </div>

    <pre class="cs-code">import yfinance as yf
import pandas as pd

def backtest(tickers: list[str], weights: list[float],
             start: str, end: str, capital: float = 10_000):
    prices = yf.download(tickers + ["SPY"], start=start, end=end)["Adj Close"]
    prices = prices.dropna()

    # Portfolio daily returns (weighted)
    port_returns = (prices[tickers] * weights).sum(axis=1) / prices[tickers].shift(1).mul(weights).sum(axis=1) - 1

    # Benchmark: SPY
    spy_returns = prices["SPY"].pct_change()

    port_value = (1 + port_returns).cumprod() * capital
    spy_value  = (1 + spy_returns).cumprod() * capital

    return port_value, spy_value</pre>

    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Monte Carlo Simulation</h3>
      <p>
        Forward-looking simulation draws from the empirical return distribution of each
        portfolio to generate 10,000 outcome paths over a user-specified horizon (e.g., 10
        or 20 years). The output is a distribution of terminal wealth values — this surfaces
        the range of outcomes, not just the expected value, which is the key insight most
        point-estimate comparisons miss.
      </p>
    </div>

    <pre class="cs-code">import numpy as np

def monte_carlo(daily_returns: pd.Series, n_years: int = 10,
                n_sims: int = 10_000, capital: float = 10_000):
    trading_days = n_years * 252
    mu    = daily_returns.mean()
    sigma = daily_returns.std()

    # Geometric Brownian Motion paths
    rand  = np.random.normal(mu, sigma, (trading_days, n_sims))
    paths = capital * np.exp(np.cumsum(rand, axis=0))

    return paths  # shape: (trading_days, n_sims)

# Key output percentiles
p10, p50, p90 = np.percentile(paths[-1], [10, 50, 90])</pre>

    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Performance Metrics</h3>
      <p>
        The summary comparison computes: Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR), Sharpe ratio
        (using 3-month T-bill rate as risk-free benchmark), maximum drawdown, and Sortino
        ratio. Both the portfolio and SPY are computed on identical date ranges and capital.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

