  <!-- CHALLENGES -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Challenges & Tradeoffs</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Modeling decisions and limitations</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Survivorship bias is real and hard to eliminate.</strong> The
        S&P 500 composition changes over time — backfilling from the current index membership
        overstates historical performance because it excludes companies that were removed
        (often for poor performance). The tool uses the SPY ETF as a proxy to partially
        mitigate this, but the active portfolios compared against it may still benefit from
        survivorship if tested on stocks that happened to survive.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Monte Carlo assumes i.i.d. returns.</strong> Geometric
        Brownian Motion treats daily returns as independently drawn from a stationary
        distribution. Real markets exhibit volatility clustering (GARCH effects) and fat
        tails. The simulation understates the probability of extreme outcomes compared to
        more sophisticated models like GARCH or regime-switching models.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Transaction costs are simplified.</strong> The current
        backtest assumes no trading friction beyond a configurable flat cost per trade.
        Bid-ask spreads, tax drag, and behavioral costs (premature selling) are not modeled —
        which means the real advantage of passive investing is likely understated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

